hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1205","Anas narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said about the major sins:\"Associating partners with Allah"
"1206","Hannad narrated to us: ‘Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from ‘Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, who said: I was asked about the two who undergo li‘an (mutual cursing) during the governorship of Mus‘ab ibn al-Zubayr—whether they should be separated—and I did not know what to say. So I went to the house of ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar and sought permission to enter. I was told he was resting. He heard my voice and called out: “Ibn Jubayr, come in! Surely you have only come for a need.” I entered and found him lying on a saddlecloth. I said: “O Abu ‘Abd al-Rahman, regarding the two who undergo li‘an—should they be separated?” He exclaimed: “Glory be to Allah! Yes! The first one to ask about this was so-and-so ibn so-and-so, who came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, what should one of us do if he sees his wife committing indecency? If he speaks, he speaks of a grave matter; if he remains silent, he remains silent about a grave matter.’ The Prophet ﷺ remained silent and did not answer him. Later, the man came again and said: ‘O Messenger of Allah, I have been afflicted with what I asked you about.’ Then Allah revealed the verses in Surah al-Nur: ‘And those who accuse their wives and have no witnesses except themselves…’ (until the end of the verses). The Prophet ﷺ then called the man, recited the verses to him, admonished him, reminded him, and informed him that the punishment of this world is lighter than the punishment of the Hereafter. The man swore: ‘By the One who sent you with the truth, I have not lied about her.’ Then the Prophet ﷺ turned to the woman, admonished her, reminded her, and informed her that the punishment of this world is lighter than the punishment of the Hereafter. She swore: ‘By the One who sent you with the truth, he is not telling the truth.’ The Prophet ﷺ then began with the man, who testified four times by Allah that he was among the truthful, and the fifth time invoked the curse of Allah upon himself if he were among the liars. Then he turned to the woman, who testified four times by Allah that he was among the liars, and the fifth time invoked the wrath of Allah upon herself if he were among the truthful. Then the Prophet ﷺ separated them.” \n(Al-Tirmidhi said:) There are narrations on this topic from Sahl ibn Sa‘d, Ibn ‘Abbas, Ibn Mas‘ud, and Hudhayfah. The hadith of Ibn ‘Umar is hasan sahih, and this is the practice followed by the scholars."
"1207","Qutaybah informed us: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Nafi‘, from Ibn ‘Umar, who said: A man pronounced li‘an against his wife, and the Prophet ﷺ separated them and attributed the child to the mother. Abu ‘Isa said: This is a hasan sahih hadith, and this is the practice followed by the scholars."
"1208","Al-Ansari informed us: Ma‘n informed us: Malik informed us, from Sa‘d ibn Ishaq ibn Ka‘b ibn ‘Ujrah, from his aunt Zaynab bint Ka‘b ibn ‘Ujrah, that al-Furay‘ah bint Malik ibn Sinan—sister of Abu Sa‘id al-Khudri—informed her that she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking to return to her family in Banu Khudrah, as her husband had gone out in search of some runaway slaves. When he reached the edge of al-Qudum, they caught up with him and killed him. She said: “I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to allow me to return to my family, for my husband had left me no home he owned nor any maintenance.” She said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Yes.’” She said: “I left, but when I was in the room or the mosque, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called me or ordered that I be called. I was summoned, and he said: ‘What did you say?’” She said: “I repeated to him the story I had told him about my husband.” He said: ‘Remain in your house until the prescribed term is fulfilled.’” She said: “I observed the waiting period there for four months and ten days.” She said: “When ‘Uthman became caliph, he sent to me and asked about the matter. I informed him, and he followed this ruling and judged accordingly.” \nMuhammad ibn Bashshar informed us: Yahya ibn Sa‘id informed us: Sa‘d ibn Ishaq ibn Ka‘b ibn ‘Ujrah informed us, with a similar narration conveying the same meaning. Abu ‘Isa said: This is a hasan sahih hadith. This is the practice followed by most of the scholars among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and others: they did not permit a woman observing ‘iddah to leave her husband’s house until her waiting period ends. This is the view of Sufyan al-Thawri, al-Shafi‘i, Ahmad, and Ishaq. \nSome scholars among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and others held that a woman may observe ‘iddah wherever she wishes, even if not in her husband’s house. Abu ‘Isa said: The first opinion is more correct."
"1209","Narrated Qutaybah ibn Sa'id, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say:\n\n\"The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters which many people do not know whether they are lawful or unlawful. Whoever avoids them in order to safeguard his religion and his honor has indeed saved himself, and whoever falls into any of them is on the verge of falling into the unlawful, just as one who grazes his flock near a sanctuary is on the verge of entering it. Indeed, every king has a sanctuary, and indeed, the sanctuary of Allah is His prohibitions.\""
"1210","and we were what was called 'brokers,' he said: 'O people of trade! Indeed the Shaitan and sin are present in the sale, so mix your sales with charity.' \\n\\nHe said: There are narrations on this topic from Al-Bara' bin 'Azib and Rifa'ah.\\n\\n[Abu 'Eisa said:] The Hadith of Qais bin Abi Gharazah (a narrator) is a Hasan Sahih Hadith.\\n"
"1211","Al-A'mash, Habib bin Abi Thabit and others reported it from Abu Wa'il, from Qais bin Abi Gharzah, from the Prophet (ﷺ). We do not know of anything from the Prophet (ﷺ) narrated by Qais other than this. \\nJam e Tirmazi Hadees: 1208 Arabic Hadees: 1208\""
"1212","The Hadith narrated by Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said regarding the major sins (al-kaba'ir): \n\n“Associating partners with Allah (shirk), disobedience to parents (‘uqūq al-wālidayn), taking a life unjustly, and bearing false witness (qawl al-zūr).” \n\nAbu Dawud (may Allah have mercy on him) noted that this hadith is narrated through Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), and there are also narrations on this topic from Abu Bakrah, Ayman ibn Khuraym, and Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them all). \n\nAbu Dawud (may Allah have mercy on him) classified the hadith of Anas as hasan sahih gharib (good, sound, and rare)."
"1213","trustworthy merchant is with the Prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs. \\n"
"1214","we do not know it except from this route, a narration of Ath-Thawri from Abu Hamzah. Abu Hamzah's name is 'Abdullah bin Jabir, and he is a Shaikh from Al-Basrah. \\nJam e Tirmazi Hadees: 1209 Arabic Hadees: 1209\""
"1215","# Translation of the Hadith\n\n**Hannād narrated to us, Qabīṣah narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Abū Ḥamzah, from al-Ḥasan, from Abū Saʿīd, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said:**\n\n**\"The truthful and trustworthy merchant shall be with the prophets, the truthful ones, and the martyrs.\"**\n\nAbū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: *\"This is a ḥasan (good) hadith. We do not know it except from this chain, from the hadith of al-Thawrī from Abū Ḥamzah.\"*\n\n*Abū Ḥamzah's name is ʿAbdullāh ibn Jābir, and he is a Baṣran shaykh.*\n\n---\n\n## Scholarly Notes\n\nThis hadith, recorded by **Imām Abū ʿĪsā Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī** (رحمه الله) in his *Jāmiʿ*, carries a profound moral and spiritual message. The Prophet ﷺ elevated the status of the merchant who combines **truthfulness (ṣidq)** and **trustworthiness (amānah)** in his commercial dealings, placing him in the company of the highest ranks of humanity: the **prophets (al-nabiyyīn)**, the **truthful ones (al-ṣiddīqīn)**, and the **martyrs (al-shuhadāʾ)**.\n\nThis hadith underscores that worldly occupations, when conducted with integrity and sincerity, are not merely permissible but can become a means of attaining the loftiest stations in the Hereafter. The merchant who refrains from deception, fulfills his obligations, and maintains honesty in trade is, in essence, embodying the prophetic character of truthfulness and trustworthiness.\n\nAl-Tirmidhī (رحمه"
"1216","Narrated Abu Dharr: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"There are three whom Allah will not look at on the Day of Judgement"
"1217","Narrated 'Umarah bin Hadid: From Shakr Al-Ghamidi that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"O Allah bless my Ummah in what they do early (in the day).\" He said: \"Whenever he (ﷺ) would dispatch a military expedition or an army"
"1218","Narrated 'Aishah:\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was wearing two thick Qitri garments on. When he would sit"
"1219","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) died while his armour was pawned for twenty Sa' of food that he got for his family.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih"
"1220","Narrated Anas: I walked to the Prophet (ﷺ) with some barley bread that has some rancid oil poured over it. The Prophet (ﷺ) had pawned his armour with a Jew for twenty Sa' of food that he got for his family. That day (he pawned it)"
"1221","Narrated 'Abbad bin Laith Al-Karabisi [Al-Basri]: \"Abdul Majid bin Wahb narrated to us"
"1222","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to the people of weights and measures: \"Indeed you have been entrusted with two matters that nations preceding you in the past were destroyed for.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] We do not know this Hadith to be Marfu' except through the narration of Husain bin Qais"
"1223","Narrated Anas bin Malik: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sold a saddle blanket and a drinking bowl. He (ﷺ) said: \"Who will buy saddle blanket and drinking bowl ?\". So a man said: \"I will take them for a Dirham.\" So the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Who will give more than a Dirham ? Who will give more that a Dirham ?\" A man agreed to give him two Dirham"
"1224","Narrated Jabir: \"A man among the Ansar decided to free a slave of his after his death. He died but he left no wealth behind beside the slave. So the Prophet (ﷺ) sold him and Nu'aim [bin 'Abdullah] bin An-Nah-ham bought him.\" Jabir said: \"He was Coptic slave who died during the first year of the leadership of Ibn Az-Zubair.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih and it has been reported through more than one route from Jabir bin 'Abdullah. This Hadith is acted upon according to some of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) and others. They did not see any harm in the sale of Mudabbar. This is the view of Ash-Shafi'i"
"1225","Narrated Ibn Mas'ud: From the Prophet (ﷺ): \"He prohibited meeting the owners of the goods.\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from 'Ali"
"1226","Narrated Abu Hurairah: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited meeting the goods being brought (to the market). If someone were to meet them and buy them"
"1227","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The dweller of the town is not to sell for the Bedouin.\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from Talhah"
"1228","Narrated Jabir: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The dweller of the town is not to sell for the Bedouin"
"1229","Narrated Abu Hurairah: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited Muhaqalah and Muzabanah. [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from Ibn 'Umar"
"1230","# Translation of the Hadith\n\n**Narrated by Jābin رضي الله عنه:**\n\nHe said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:\n\n**\"A townsman (ḥāḍir) shall not act as an agent to sell on behalf of a bedouin (bādin). Leave the people, for Allah provides some of them through others.\"**\n\n---\n\n**Imām Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:**\n\nThe Hadith of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه is a *ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ* Hadith. And the Hadith of Jābir رضي الله عنه on this matter is also a *ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ* Hadith.\n\nThe practice (al-ʿamal) according to this Hadith is held by some of the People of Knowledge from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and others, who disliked (karihū) that a townsman should sell on behalf of a bedouin. However, some of them permitted (rukhiṣa) that a townsman may purchase on behalf of a bedouin.\n\n**Imām al-Shāfiʿī** said: It is disliked (yukraha) for a townsman to sell on behalf of a bedouin; however, if he does sell, the sale is valid (al-bayʿ jāʾiz)."
"1231","Abu Ayyash asked Sa'd regarding white wheat in exchange for barley: which of them was better ? He said the white, then he forbade that. Sa'd said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) being asked about selling dried dates for ripe dates and he said to those present: Will the fresh dates shrink when they are dry ? They said yes, so he forbade that.' \\nJam e Tirmazi Hadees: 1225 Arabic Hadees: 1225\""
"1232","Narrated Ibn 'Umar:\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited selling date-palms until they have blossomed"
"1233","With this (same as no. 1226) chain:\"The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited selling ears (of grain) until they have whitened (shown their kernals) and are safe from blight"
"1234","Narrated Anas: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited selling grapes until they appear and selling grains until they become firm.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Gharib"
"1235","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited the sale of Habalil-Habalah.\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from 'Abdullah bin 'Abbas and Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri [Abu 'Eisa said:] The Hadith of Ibn 'Umar is Hasan Sahih Hadith. This is acted upon according to the people of knowledge. And Hababil-Habalah is the offspring of the offspring (of an animal). It is an invalid sale according to the people of knowledge and it is type of Gharar sale. Shu'bah reported this Hadith from Ayyub"
"1236","Narrated Abu Hurairah: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited the Gharar sale"
"1237","Narrated Abu Hurairah: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited two sales in one.\" There are narrations on this topic from 'Abdullah bin 'Amr"
"1238","Abū ʿĪsā (may Allāh have mercy on him) narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Abū Usāmah informed us, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) prohibited the sale of *al-gharar* (uncertainty/risk) and the sale of *al-ḥaṣāh* (the sale by casting a stone). He (Abū ʿĪsā) said: In this chapter, there are narrations from Ibn ʿUmar, Ibn ʿAbbās, Abū Saʿīd, and Anas (may Allāh be pleased with them all). Abū ʿĪsā said: The hadith of Abū Hurayrah is a *ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ* (good and authentic) hadith. The practice among the scholars is in accordance with this hadith; they disapproved of the sale of *al-gharar*. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allāh have mercy on him) said: Among the sales of *al-gharar* are the sale of fish still in the water, the sale of a runaway slave, the sale of a bird in the sky, and similar types of transactions. The meaning of the sale of *al-ḥaṣāh* is that the seller says to the buyer: \"When I cast the stone toward you, the sale between us is binding.\" This is similar to the sale of *al-munābadhah* (a pre-Islamic practice where goods were sold by tossing), and this was among the sales of the people of *al-Jāhiliyyah* (the pre-Islamic era of ignorance)."
"1239","Narrated Hakim b. Hizam: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited me from selling what was not with me.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan. He said: There is something on this topic from 'Abdullah bin 'Umar. Ishaq bin Mansur said: \"I said to Ahmad: 'What is the meaning of the prohibition from a loan along with a sale? He said: 'That he gives him a loan and then he makes a sale to him greater then it's actual worth. And"
"1240","Qutaybah narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Hakim ibn Hizam, who said:\n\n\"I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: 'A man comes to me asking me for something for sale that I do not possess. Should I buy it for him from the market and then sell it to him?' He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Do not sell what is not with you.'\"\n\nHe (the narrator) said: \"And in this chapter (there is also a narration) from Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him).\""
"1241","Narrated Hakim b. Hizam: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited me from selling what was not with me.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] Waki' reported this Hadith from Yazid bin Ibrahim"
"1242","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited selling the Wala' and conferring it.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. We do not know of it except as a narration of 'Abdullah bin Dinar"
"1243","Narrated Samurah: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited bartering animals on credit.\" He said: There are narration on this topic from Ibn 'Abbas"
"1244","Narrated Jabir: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Animals [two for one] are not proper on credit"
"1245","Narrated Jabir: \"A slave came to give the pledge to the Prophet (ﷺ) for Hijrah"
"1246","Narrated 'Ubadah bin As-Samit: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Gold for gold"
"1247","Narrated Nafi': \"Ibn 'Umar and I went to Abu Sa'eed and he narrated to us: 'the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said - and I heard him with these [two] ears: \"Do not sell gold for gold except kind for kind"
"1248","# Translation of the Hadith\n\nSuwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, who said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, who said: Sufyan informed us, from Khalid al-Hadha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said:\n\n**\"Gold is to be exchanged for gold, like for like; silver is to be exchanged for silver, like for like; dates are to be exchanged for dates, like for like; wheat is to be exchanged for wheat, like for like; salt is to be exchanged for salt, like for like; and barley is to be exchanged for barley, like for like. Whoever gives more or seeks more has indeed engaged in riba (usury). Sell gold for silver however you wish, hand to hand (on the spot); sell wheat for dates however you wish, hand to hand; and sell barley for dates however you wish, hand to hand.\"**\n\nHe (Abu 'Isa) said: **\"In this chapter there are narrations from Abu Sa'id, Abu Hurayrah, Bilal, and Anas.\"**\n\nAbu 'Isa said: **\"The Hadith of 'Ubadah is a hasan sahih (good and authentic) Hadith.\"**\n\nHe said: **\"Some of them narrated this Hadith from Khalid with this chain of transmission and said: 'Sell wheat for barley however you wish, hand to hand.'\"**\n\nHe said: **\"Some of them narrated this Hadith from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from 'Ubadah, from the Prophet ﷺ, and added in it that Khalid said: Abu Qilabah said: 'Sell wheat for barley however you wish,' and he mentioned the Hadith.\"**\n\nHe said: **\"The practice upon this is according to the people of knowledge. They do not consider it permissible to sell wheat for wheat except like for like, and barley for barley except like for like. However, when the categories differ, there is no harm in selling with disparity, provided it is hand to hand (on the spot). This is the position of the majority of the people of knowledge from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and others, and it is the position of Sufyan al-Thawri, al-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq.\"**\n\nAl-Shafi'i said: **\"The proof for that is the statement of the Prophet ﷺ: 'Sell barley for wheat however you wish, hand to hand.'\"**\n\nAbu 'Isa said: **\"A group of the people of knowledge disliked that wheat be sold for barley except like for like, and this is the position of Malik ibn Anas. However, the first position is more correct.\"**"
"1249","Narrated Ibn Shihab: From Malik bin Aws bin Hadathan that he said: \"I once said: 'Who can change some Dirham?' So Talhah bin 'Ubaidullah - and he was with 'Umar bin Al-Khattab - said: \"Leave your gold with us"
"1250","Narrated Salim: From his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever purchases a date-palm after it has been pollinated then its fruits are for the one who sold it"
"1251","# Hadith on Riba (Usury) in Currency and Commodity Exchanges\n\n**Narrated by Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan:**\n\nHe said: \"I came saying, 'Who will exchange the dirhams [for me]?' Thereupon, Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah — may Allah be pleased with him — who was in the presence of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab — may Allah be pleased with him — said: 'Show us your gold, and then come to us when our servant arrives, and we will give you your silver.'\"\n\nWhereupon **'Umar ibn al-Khattab** — may Allah be pleased with him — said: \"Nay! By Allah, you shall either give him his silver [in return for his gold] or return his gold to him. For indeed, the **Messenger of Allah** — peace and blessings of Allah be upon him — said:\n\n> *'Silver in exchange for gold is riba (usury), except hand to hand [i.e., on the spot]. Wheat in exchange for wheat is riba, except hand to hand. Barley in exchange for barley is riba, except hand to hand. Dates in exchange for dates are riba, except hand to hand.'*\"\n\n**Abu 'Isa [al-Tirmidhi]** said: This is a **hasan sahih** (good and authentic) hadith. The practice upon this is held by the people of knowledge. His statement — peace and blessings of Allah be upon him — *\"except hand to hand\"* means: **immediate exchange on the spot** (*yadan bi-yadin*).\n\n---\n\n### Scholarly Notes:\n\nThis hadith constitutes a foundational text in the Islamic jurisprudence of financial transactions (*mu'amalat*), establishing the prohibition of **riba al-fadl** (usury by excess) in the exchange of homogeneous commodities of the same genus (*jins*), and stipulating the condition of **immediate, on-the-spot exchange** (*taqabud*) as a necessary requirement for the permissibility of such transactions. The scholars of the four madhhabs have relied upon this hadith extensively in their rulings pertaining to currency exchange (*sarf*) and the sale of staple foodstuffs."
"1252","Narrated Hakim b. Hizam: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Both the buyer and the seller retain the option as long as they have not separated. If they spoke the truth and clarified (any defects or conditions)"
"1253","# Translation of the Hadith\n\n**Wāṣil ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Kūfī narrated to us**, saying: **Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us**, from **Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd**, from **Nāfiʿ**, from **Ibn ʿUmar** (may Allāh be pleased with both of them), who said:\n\n> \"I heard the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) say: **'The two parties to a sale have the option (of annulling the transaction) so long as they have not separated, or (until) one of them chooses (to finalize it).'**\"\n\nHe (the narrator) said: **Whenever Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) concluded a sale while he was sitting, he would stand up so that the sale would become binding upon him.**\n\n**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** And in this chapter (there are narrations) from **Abū Barzah**, **Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām**, **ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās**, **ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr**, **Samurah**, and **Abū Hurayrah** (may Allāh be pleased with them all).\n\n**Abū ʿĪsā said:** The Hadith of Ibn ʿUmar is a **ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ** (good and authentic) hadith. The practice (established) upon this is held by some of the people of knowledge from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and others. This is the position of **al-Shāfiʿī**, **Aḥmad**, and **Isḥāq**, who said: **\"Separation is by the bodies, not by speech.\"**\n\nSome of the people of knowledge said that the meaning of the Prophet's statement (peace and blessings be upon him) **\"so long as they have not separated\"** refers to **separation by speech (i.e., verbal declaration)**. However, **the first opinion is more correct**, because **Ibn ʿUmar** (may Allāh be pleased with him) is the one who narrated this from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he is **most knowledgeable of the meaning of what he narrated**. It is reported from him that whenever he wished to make a sale binding, he would walk (a short distance) so that it would become binding upon him. And similarly, it has been reported from **Abū Barzah al-Aslamī** (may Allāh be pleased with him)."
"1254","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"They (the two) are not separate from a sale except in agreement. [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Gharib"
"1255","Narrated Jabir: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) gave a Bedouin the option after a sale.\" This Hadith is Hasan Gharib"
"1256","Narrated Anas: That there was a man who was not very sensible and he would make purchases. So his family came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: \"O Messenger of Allah! Stop him (from making purchases).\" So Allah's Prophet (ﷺ) called him to prohibit him"
"1257","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever purchased an animal that has not been milked"
"1258","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever purchases an animal that has not been milked"
"1259","Narrated Jabir bin ‘Abdullah : That he sold a camel to the Prophet (ﷺ) and made a condition that he could ride it to (return to) his family. [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. It has been reported through other routes from Jabir. This is acted upon according to some of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) and others. They consider it allowed to make a condition in a sale when it is one condition. This is the view of Ahmad and Ishaq. Some of the people of knowledge said that it is not allowed to make a condition in a sale"
"1260","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): \"A riding animal can be ridden while it is pawned"
"1261","Narrated Fadalah bin 'Ubaidah:\\n\\n On the Day of Khaibar I purchased a necklace that contained gold and jewels for twelve Dinar. I separated it and found that it was worth more than twelve Dinar. I mentioned that to the Prophet (ﷺ) and he said: 'Do not sell it until it is separated.' \\nJam e Tirmazi Hadees: 1255 Arabic Hadees: 1255\""
"1262","Narrated Al-Aswad: From 'Aishah that she wanted to purchase Barirah"
"1263","Narrated Fadālah ibn ʿUbayd (may Allah be pleased with him): \n\"I purchased a necklace on the day of Khaybar for twelve dinars. It contained gold and beads. When I had it appraised, I found its value to be more than twelve dinars. I mentioned this to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: 'It shall not be sold until it is appraised (separately).'\""
"1264","Qutaybah narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Abu Shuja', Sa'id ibn Yazid, with this chain of transmission, something similar to it. Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a hasan sahih hadith. The practice according to this is held by some of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and others. They did not permit the sale of a sword adorned with gold or a belt embellished with silver, or the like, in exchange for dirhams, until it is distinguished and separated. This is the position of Ibn al-Mubarak, al-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq. And some of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and others, have granted a concession in that regard."
"1265","and I sold one of them for a Dinar. So I returned with the sheep and the Dinar to the Prophet (ﷺ), and I mentioned what had happened and he said: 'May Allah bless you in your business dealings.' After that we went to Kunasah in Al-Kufah, and he made tremendous profits. He was among the wealthiest of the people in Al-Kufah. \\nJam e Tirmazi Hadees: 1258 Arabic Hadees: 1258\""
"1266","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"When the penalty (of blood money) goes to a Mukatab"
"1267","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Darimi, narrated to us Habban, who was Abu Habib al-Basri, narrated to us Harun al-Awar al-Muqri', who was the reciter, narrated to us al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit, from Abu Labid, from 'Urwah al-Bariqi, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave me a dinar to purchase a sheep for him. I purchased two sheep for him, then sold one of them for a dinar and brought the other sheep and the dinar to the Prophet (ﷺ). I informed him of what had transpired, and he (ﷺ) invoked blessings upon him, saying:\n\n**\"Bārak Allāhu laka fī ṣafqat yaminik\"** *(May Allah bless the transaction of your right hand).*\n\nAfter that, he would proceed to the marketplace of Kūfah and achieve tremendous profit, eventually becoming among the wealthiest of the people of Kūfah."
"1268","Narrated Umm Salamah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you (women) has a Mukatab ho has with him what will fulfill (the Kitabah) then observe Hijab from him.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. And the meaning of this Hadith according to the people of knowledge is that of caution. They say that the Mukatab is not freed"
"1269","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whichever person becomes bankrupt"
"1270","Narrated Abu Al-Waddak: That Abu Sa'eed said: \"We had some wine that belonged to an orphan. When Al-Ma'idah was revealed I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about it"
"1271","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Fulfill the trust for the one who entrusted you"
"1272","Narrated Abu Umamah: \"During the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage"
"1273","# Translation of the Hadith\n\n**Narrated by Abu Sa'eed (may Allah be pleased with him):**\n\nHe said: \"We had in our possession some wine belonging to an orphan. When Surah al-Ma'idah was revealed, I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about it, saying, 'It belongs to an orphan.' He ﷺ said: **'Pour it out.'**\"\n\nHe (the narrator) added: \"In this chapter (on this subject), there is also a narration from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him).\"\n\nAbu 'Eesaa (al-Tirmidhi) said: \"The hadith of Abu Sa'eed is a *hasan* (good) hadith. Similar narrations have been reported from the Prophet ﷺ through other chains. Some of the scholars held this view and disliked that wine be made into vinegar. What was disliked in this matter—and Allah knows best—is that a Muslim should have wine in his house until it becomes vinegar. However, some of them permitted the vinegar of wine if it is found to have already become vinegar.\"\n\n**Note:** Abu al-Waddak's name is Jabr ibn Nawf."
"1274","Abū Kurayb narrated to us, Ṭalq ibn Ghannām narrated to us, from Sharīq and Qays, from Abū Ḥuṣayn, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Render back the trust to the one who entrusted you, and do not betray the one who betrays you.\" Abū ʿĪsā said: This is a ḥasan gharīb hadith. Some scholars have followed this hadith and said: If a man has something belonging to another and he takes it, and then something of his falls into the possession of that other person, he is not entitled to withhold from him an amount equivalent to what was taken from him. Some scholars among the Successors have granted a concession in this matter. This is the view of al-Thawrī, who said: If he owes him dirhams and dinars fall into his possession, he is not entitled to withhold the dinars in place of the dirhams, unless dirhams fall into his possession belonging to him; in that case, he may then withhold from the dirhams an amount equivalent to what is owed to him."
"1275","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Do not go out to meet the market (caravan)"
"1276","Narrated 'Abdullah bin Mas'ud: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever takes a false oath to deprive a Muslim of his wealth"
"1277","Narrated Ibn Mas'ud: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When the two parties (in a deal) disagree then the seller's statement is taken as valid"
"1278","Narrated Abu Al-Minhal: From Iyas bin 'Abd al-Muzani who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited selling water.\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from Jabir"
"1279","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Do not withhold surplus water so that it is prevented from the pasture.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. Abu Al-Munhal's name is 'Abdur-Rahman bin Mut'im"
"1280","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited studding the stallion.\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from Abu Hurairah"
"1281","Narrated Anas bin Malik: \"A man from (the tribe of) Kilab asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about studding a stallion and he prohibited it. So he said: 'O Messenger of Allah! We stud the stallions so that we get honorarium (from the owners of the female horse)!' So he permitted it for honorarium.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Gharib"
"1282","Narrated Rafi' b. Khadij: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The earnings of the cupper is filth"
"1283","Narrated Abu Mas'ud Al-Ansari: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited the price of a dog"
"1284","# Translation of the Hadith\n\n**Narrated by Anas ibn Mālik (may Allāh be pleased with him):**\n\nA man from the tribe of Kilāb asked the Prophet ﷺ about the stud fee of a male animal (ʿasb al-faḥl). The Prophet ﷺ prohibited it. The man said, \"O Messenger of Allāh, we allow the male animal to cover (our females), and we are honoured (for doing so).\" The Prophet ﷺ then granted him a dispensation regarding the act of honouring (i.e., he permitted the acceptance of a gift or token of gratitude, as opposed to a fixed fee).\n\n**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** This is a *ḥasan gharīb* hadith. We do not know it except through the narration of Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥumayd from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.\n\n---\n\n### Notes on Key Terms:\n\n- **ʿAsb al-faḥl** (عَسْبُ الفَحْلِ): The fee or payment charged for the service of a male animal (stud fee). The Prophet ﷺ initially prohibited this as it resembled a form of transaction involving the reproductive capacity of an animal, which is impermissible to sell.\n- **Nuṭriq al-faḥl** (نُطْرِقُ الفَحْلَ): \"We allow the male to cover/mate\" — referring to the practice of lending one's stud animal for breeding purposes.\n- **Nukramu** (نُكْرَمُ): \"We are honoured/given a gift\" — the man explained that people would offer them a gift or token of appreciation, not a fixed price.\n- **Rukhaṣa fī al-karāma** (رُخْصَةٌ فِي"
"1285","Narrated Anas: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was cupped. Abu Talhah did the cupping. So he ordered that he be given two Sa' of food"
"1286","Narrated Jabir: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited the price of the dog and the cat.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] There is some confusion (Idtirab) in the chain for this Hadith. The price of a cat is not correct. This Hadith has been reported from Al-A'mash"
"1287","Narrated Jabir: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited eating the cat and from its price.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Gharib. We do not know of any major (known) narrators who reports from 'Umar bin Zaid (one of the narrators) besides 'Abdur-Razzaq"
"1288","Narrated Abu Al-Muhazzim: From Abu Hurairah who said: \"The price of a dog was prohibited"
"1289","Narrated Abu Umamah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Do not sell the (slave) female singers"
"1290","Narrated Abu Ayyub: \"I narrated heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: 'Whoever seperates a mother from her child"
"1291","Narrated 'Ali : \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave me two boys who were brothers"
"1292","Narrated 'Aishah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) judged: \"The produce is for the responsible one.\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. This Hadith has been reported through routes other than this"
"1293","Narrated 'Aishah: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) judged that the produce is produce is for the responsible one.\" [He said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih"
"1294","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever enters an orchard then let him eat"
"1295","Narrated Rafi' bin 'Amr: \"I was throwing stones at a date-palm belonging to some of the Ansar. They took me along with them to the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: \"O Rafi'! Why were you throwing stones at their date-palm?'\" He said: \"I said: 'Out of hunger"
"1296","Narrated to us by Abū Salamah, Yaḥyā ibn Khalaf, who informed us ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī al-Muqaddamī, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ruled that al-kharāj (yield or profit) is tied to liability (al-ḍamān). \n\nHe (the narrator) said: This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīh gharīb hadith from the narration of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. \n\nAbū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl (al-Bukhārī) considered this hadith gharīb from the narration of ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī. \n\nI asked: Do you see it as tadlīs (concealment in the chain)? He said: No. \n\nAbū ʿĪsā said: Muslim ibn Khālid al-Zanjī also narrated this hadith from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, and Jarīr also narrated it from Hishām. \n\nThe hadith of Jarīr is said to involve tadlīs; Jarīr practiced tadlīs in it, for he did not hear it directly from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. \n\nThe explanation of \"al-kharāj bi al-ḍamān\" (profit goes with liability) is: A man purchases a slave, derives benefit from him, then discovers a defect and returns him to the seller. In such a case, the yield (ghallah) belongs to the buyer, because if the slave had perished, the loss would have fallen upon the buyer’s property. \n\nSimilar cases in jurisprudence follow this principle: wherever there is liability (ḍamān), the profit (kharāj) accompanies it. \n\nAbū ʿĪsā said: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl considered this hadith gharīb from the narration of ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī. I asked: Do you see it as tadlīs? He said: No."
"1297","Narrated Jabir: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited Al-Muhaqalah"
"1298","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever buys food"
"1299","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"None of you is to sell over the sale of others"
"1300","Narrated Anas: From Abu Talhah that he said: \"O Prophet of Allah! I had purchased some wine for the orphans under my care. He said: 'Spill out the wine"
"1301","Narrated Anas bin Malik: \"I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) 'Can wine be used for vinegar?' He said: 'No'\" [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih"
"1302","Narrated Anas bin Malik: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed ten involved in wine: The one who presses it"
"1303","Narrated Samurah bin Jundab: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you comes upon livestock"
"1304","Narrated Jabir bin ‘Abdullah : That during the Year of the Conquest"
"1305","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Ours is not a bad example: The one who takes back his gift is like the dog who takes back his vomit.\" [He said:] On this topic"
"1306","Narrated 'Amr bin Shu'aib: That he heard Tawus narrating from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas"
"1307","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: From Zaid bin Thabit that the Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited Al-Muhalaqah and Al-Muzabanah"
"1308","Narrated Abu Hurairah:\\n\\nThat the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) permitted Al-'Araya in cases less than five Wasq. Or similar. \\nJam e Tirmazi Hadees: 1301 Arabic Hadees: 1301\""
"1309","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: From Zaid bin Thabit that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) permitted selling in Al-'Araya by estimating it. [Abu 'Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. The Hadith of Abu Hurairah is Hasan Sahih. And this is acted upon according to some of the people of knowledge. Among them Ash-Shafi'i"
"1310","# Translation of the Hadith\n\n**Hannād narrated to us, ʿAbdah narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from Zayd ibn Thābit, that the Prophet ﷺ prohibited *al-muḥāqalah* and *al-muzābanah*, except that he permitted the people of *al-ʿarāyā* to sell them at the equivalent of their estimated yield.**\n\nHe said: \"And in this chapter there are narrations from Abū Hurayrah and Jābir.\"\n\nAbū ʿĪsā said: \"The Ḥadīth of Zayd ibn Thābit — thus did Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrate this Ḥadīth. And Ayyūb, ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, and Mālik ibn Anas narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet ﷺ prohibited *al-muḥāqalah* and *al-muzābanah*. And with this chain of narration, from Ibn ʿUmar, from Zayd ibn Thābit, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he granted a concession regarding *al-ʿarāyā*. And this is more authentic (*aṣaḥḥ*) than the Ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq.\"\n\n---\n\n**Note:** *Al-muḥāqalah* refers to the sale of grain in the field for grain harvested, and *al-muzābanah* refers to the sale of fresh dates on the tree for dried dates by measure — both forms of sale involving *gharar* (uncertainty). *Al-ʿarāyā* refers to a concession allowing the sale of dates on the tree by estimation for the purpose of eating fresh dates."
"1311","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Do not practice An-Najsh.\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from Ibn 'Umar and Anas. [Abu 'Eisa said:] The Hadith of Abu Hurairah is a Hasan Sahih Hadith. This is acted upon according to the people of knowledge"
"1312","Narrated Suwaid bin Qais: \"Makhrafah Al-'Abdi and I brought linens from Hajar. The Prophet (ﷺ) came to us in bargain with us with some pants. There was someone with me who weighed (the goods) to determine the value. So the the Prophet (ﷺ) said to the one weighing: 'Weigh and add more.'\" [He said:] There are narrations on this topic from Jabir and Abu Hurairah. [Abu 'Eisa said:] The Hadith of Suwaid is a Hasan Sahih Hadith. The people of knowledge consider it recommended to add more when weighing. Shu'bah reported this Hadith from Simak"
"1313","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever grants respite to an indigent or alleviates it for him"
"1314","Narrated Abu Mas'ud: That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"A man among those before you was called to reckon and nothing good was found with him. Except that he was a wealthy man so he used to mix with the pople and he would tell his servant to be lenient with the insolvent. So Allah"
"1315","Narrated Abu Hurairah: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Procrastination (in paying a debt) by a rich person is oppression. So if your debt is transferred from your debtor to a rich debtor"
"1316","Narrated Ibn 'Umar: That the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Procrastination (in paying a debt) by a rich person is oppression. So if your debt is transfered from your debtor you should agree"
"1317","Narrated Abu Hurairah: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prohibited sales of Al-Munabadhah and Al-Mulamasah.\" He said: There are narrations on this topic from Abu Sa'eed and Ibn 'Umar. [Abu 'Eisa said:] The Hadith is Abu Hurairah is a Hasan Sahih Hadith. And the meaning of (Munabadhah) in this Hadith is when it is said: \"When I throw something to you then the sale between you and I is concluded.\" And Al-Mulamasah is that he says: \"When you touch something then the sale is concluded.\" Even if he did not see it at all"
"1318","Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: \"When the Prophet (ﷺ) arrived in Al-Madinah"
"1319","Narrated Jabir bin ‘Abdullah : That Allah's Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever has a partner in an orchard"
"1320","Narrated Abu Az-Zubair: From Jabir that the Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited Al-Muhaqalah"
"1321","Narrated Anas: \"Prices became excessive during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"
